course: dictionary
lesson: 7110
title: Everyday words like peckish
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal. People use them in chatty talk with friends and family, not usually in a formal letter or an essay. «I'm feeling a bit peckish» is one of these. Peckish means a little bit hungry, so the speaker wants a snack, not a big meal.
explain: Context helps you work out the meaning. The words «a bit» show that the hunger is small, and the idea of a snack fits with it. Try the swap test: «I'm feeling a bit hungry» still makes sense, so peckish must mean a little hungry.
explain: Peckish is mostly used in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. If you hear it somewhere else, the words around it are your best clues, and a dictionary can confirm the meaning.
fact: Peckish is an informal word, so you will hear it in everyday speech more than in formal writing.
fact: «Peckish» means a little bit hungry.
fact: Peckish is used mainly in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: The word is said to come from birds pecking at food, which is a fun way to remember it.
fact: «I'm a bit peckish» can sound polite, because it makes hunger sound small, even when someone is quite hungry.
exercise: «I'm feeling a bit peckish, so I'll have a biscuit.» What does peckish mean? | Completely full | Fast asleep | *A little bit hungry | Very angry
exercise: Ella's friend in Sydney says, «I'm a bit peckish.» What does she want? | A long bath | To go to bed | A big roast dinner | *A small snack
exercise: Why might a writer choose peckish in a story? | *To give the story a friendly, casual tone | To say a character has been ill for a week | To make the story sound like a science report | To describe a very cold day
test: «I'm a bit peckish» tells you the speaker is | *A little bit hungry, and speaking casually | Sleepy and ready for bed | Bored of the film | Very ill and needs a doctor
test: Where would you most likely hear the word peckish? | In a weather report about snow in Canada | *In a chat with friends in Australia | In a formal letter to a bank | In a maths lesson about fractions
exam: Which sentence uses peckish in an informal way? | *«I'm feeling a bit peckish, so I'll grab a biscuit.» | «My appetite is currently diminished.» | «I am slightly hungry, so I shall have a light refreshment.» | «The patient requires nourishment.»
